--TEST--
A function that waits is called the same way everywhere — at the top level, from another function, as a field of `all` and as a `map` callback — and all four positions answer the same value, so no call site is coloured by the wait inside it — `rule:concurrency/one-scheduler`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/no-colouring-i-o-just-waits
// The agreement shape (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): the assertion is that the four
// positions agree, not what any one of them answered. A language that grew a second
// spelling for a waiting call — a promise at the top level, a value inside a task —
// fails here while every individual line still looks right.
class Slow {
    public static function value(int $n): int
    {
        Core\Time::sleep(1ms);
        return $n * 3;
    }

    // A caller that does not wait itself, calling one that does. There is no third
    // thing to write here: the call is the same call.
    public static function twice(int $n): int
    {
        return Slow::value($n) + Slow::value($n);
    }
}

int $agreed = 0;
int $asked = 0;

int $plain = Slow::value(2);
$asked += 1;
if ($plain == 6) {
    $agreed += 1;
}

int $nested = Slow::twice(1);
$asked += 1;
if ($nested == 6) {
    $agreed += 1;
}

var $group = Core\Task::all({one: fn(): int => Slow::value(2)});
$asked += 1;
if ($group->one == 6) {
    $agreed += 1;
}

var $mapped = Core\Task::map([2, 2], fn(int $n): int => Slow::value($n));
$asked += 1;
if ($mapped[0] == 6 && $mapped[1] == 6) {
    $agreed += 1;
}

echo $agreed, " of ", $asked, " positions answered the same", "\n";
--EXPECT--
4 of 4 positions answered the same
